{"address":"201 Robert S Kerr Ave, Oklahoma City, OK 73102","context":"buying a home","capturedAt":"2026-06-27","events":[{"t":7,"event":{"type":"status","text":"Locating address..."}},{"t":461,"event":{"type":"location","lat":35.469915100255,"lng":-97.516443961841,"display_name":"201 ROBERT S KERR AVE, OKLAHOMA CITY, OK, 73102"}},{"t":461,"event":{"type":"status","text":"Determining relevant risk dimensions..."}},{"t":1362,"event":{"type":"agents_selected","agents":["wildfire","flood","air_quality","weather","history"]}},{"t":1362,"event":{"type":"agent_running","dimension":"wildfire"}},{"t":1362,"event":{"type":"agent_running","dimension":"flood"}},{"t":1362,"event":{"type":"agent_running","dimension":"air_quality"}},{"t":1362,"event":{"type":"agent_running","dimension":"weather"}},{"t":1362,"event":{"type":"agent_running","dimension":"history"}},{"t":1362,"event":{"type":"status","text":"Running 5 risk agents in parallel..."}},{"t":4262,"event":{"type":"risk_update","result":{"dimension":"air_quality","score":2,"level":"Moderate","headline":"Current AQI near 35.47N, -97.52W (Oklahoma City) is 64 (Moderate), driven by PM2.5.","details":["Fine particle pollution (PM2.5) is in the Moderate range (AQI 64), which may mildly affect people who are unusually sensitive to air pollution.","Ozone (AQI 26) and coarse particles PM10 (AQI 41) are both in the Good range and are not expected to cause health effects for the general population.","Unusually sensitive individuals (especially those with asthma, COPD, or heart disease) should consider limiting prolonged or heavy outdoor exertion, particularly near busy roads or industrial areas.","For most healthy adults and children, normal outdoor activities are acceptable, but reducing exposure by avoiding smoke, dust, or heavy traffic can further minimize risk.","Conditions are currently stable and well below levels associated with wildfire smoke emergencies or significant inhalation risk."],"sources":["EPA AirNow"],"geojson":null,"threat_vector":null,"data_as_of":"2026-06-27 17:57 UTC","agent_skipped":false,"skip_reason":null}}},{"t":4947,"event":{"type":"risk_update","result":{"dimension":"weather","score":2,"level":"Moderate","headline":"No active alerts, but summer convective storms and rapid changes remain possible in the Oklahoma City area.","details":["No current NWS watches, warnings, or advisories are in effect for Oklahoma City, indicating no imminent hazardous weather officially flagged at this time.","Central Oklahoma is climatologically prone to severe thunderstorms, heavy rain, hail, and tornadoes, especially from spring into early summer, so conditions can escalate quickly even without existing alerts.","Daytime heating, frontal boundaries, or outflow from distant storms can trigger rapid thunderstorm development; new Severe Thunderstorm or Tornado Watches/Warnings may be issued with only short lead time.","If recent days have been hot and dry, fire danger can increase even without a Red Flag Warning, particularly in grassy or brushy areas exposed to wind.","Monitor updated NWS forecasts and radar through the day and be prepared to act if new alerts (Severe Thunderstorm Warning, Tornado Watch/Warning, or Flash Flood Warning) are issued."],"sources":["NOAA National Weather Service"],"geojson":null,"threat_vector":null,"data_as_of":"2026-06-27 17:57 UTC","agent_skipped":false,"skip_reason":null}}},{"t":5151,"event":{"type":"risk_update","result":{"dimension":"wildfire","score":2,"level":"Moderate","headline":"No active fires detected nearby, but strong winds and regional fuels support a moderate wildfire risk.","details":["NASA FIRMS VIIRS shows 0 active fire pixels within 100 km in the last 24 hours, indicating no currently detected wildfire activity in the immediate region.","NIFC reports 0 named fire perimeters within 100 miles, so there are no large, ongoing incident fires threatening the location at this time.","Current winds are 24.9 km/h from 200° with gusts up to 39.2 km/h, which are strong enough to drive rapid fire spread and spotting if an ignition occurs.","The location near Oklahoma City is surrounded by mixed grassland, brush, and urban interface; these fuels can support fast-moving grass and brush fires under windy conditions, especially during dry periods.","Despite the absence of active detections, the combination of open fuels, developed areas, and elevated wind speeds means any new ignition (e.g., from equipment, power lines, or human activity) could quickly become hazardous."],"sources":["NASA FIRMS VIIRS NRT","NIFC Active Fire Perimeters"],"geojson":null,"threat_vector":null,"data_as_of":"2026-06-27 17:57 UTC","agent_skipped":false,"skip_reason":null}}},{"t":6892,"event":{"type":"risk_update","result":{"dimension":"flood","score":3,"level":"High","headline":"Flood risk is elevated due to lack of mapped data in a county with known significant flood history.","details":["No FEMA flood zone is mapped at this exact point, indicating it may lie outside a detailed flood study area or in a data gap rather than confirming low risk.","The location is in Oklahoma County, OK, where repeated severe storm and flood events have historically affected both mapped and unmapped areas.","Absence of zone data means potential exposure to local drainage, ponding, and urban flooding that would not appear on riverine/coastal flood maps.","Without a defined FEMA zone (AE, X, etc.), insurance and building decisions should assume at least moderate to high risk until site-specific elevation and drainage are evaluated.","Local topography, soil conditions, and stormwater infrastructure should be reviewed through surveys or local floodplain management offices to refine this preliminary risk rating."],"sources":["FEMA NFHL","OpenFEMA Disaster Declarations"],"geojson":null,"threat_vector":null,"data_as_of":"2026-06-27 17:57 UTC","agent_skipped":false,"skip_reason":null}}},{"t":7919,"event":{"type":"risk_update","result":{"dimension":"history","score":2,"level":"Moderate","headline":"Oklahoma County has 48 federal disaster declarations since 1974 (0.9/year), with Fire as the most frequent hazard (16 events).","details":["Overall activity is below the U.S. county average (0.9 vs. ~1.5 declarations/year), but the county has seen a clear rise since 2000, with 32 of 48 declarations occurring from 2000 onward.","Fire is the dominant hazard type (16 events), including recent FMAG fires such as DR-5459 Hefner Fire (2023-03-31) and DR-5562 Luther Fire (2025-03-14), indicating recurring wildland/urban-interface vulnerability.","The 2000s (17 declarations) and 2010s (15) show strong clustering compared with the 1970s–1980s (2 each) and 1990s (5), suggesting growing exposure and/or more frequent hazard events in recent decades.","Multiple 2025 declarations in close succession—DR-5562 Luther Fire (2025-03-14), DR-4862 Severe Storms, Straight-Line Winds, Tornadoes, and Flooding (2025-03-18), and DR-4866 Wildfires and Straight-Line Winds (2025-05-21)—highlight systemic vulnerability to compound wind, fire, and storm hazards.","The most recent major event is DR-4866 Wildfires and Straight-Line Winds (declared 2025-05-21), following the February 2021 severe winter storms (DR-3555 and DR-4587) that exposed sensitivity to prolonged ice, power disruption, and cold-weather impacts."],"sources":["OpenFEMA Disaster Declarations","US Census Bureau Geocoder"],"geojson":{"total":48,"county":"Oklahoma County","state":"","by_type":{"Fire":16,"Severe Storm":15,"Severe Ice Storm":10,"Flood":4,"Tornado":2,"Human Cause":1},"by_decade":{"1970s":2,"1980s":2,"1990s":5,"2000s":17,"2010s":15,"2020s":7},"year_range":[1974,2025],"notable":[{"number":"DR-4866","title":"WILDFIRES AND STRAIGHT-LINE WINDS","type":"Fire","date":"2025-05-21"},{"number":"DR-4862","title":"SEVERE STORMS, STRAIGHT-LINE WINDS, TORNADOES, AND FLOODING","type":"Severe Storm","date":"2025-03-18"},{"number":"DR-5562","title":"LUTHER FIRE","type":"Fire","date":"2025-03-14"},{"numbe